ue above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \n- You hold A♥ 6♥, and the board shows 2♥ 4♥ 8♦ 8♠ 7♠.  \n- The final board includes three hearts (2♥, 4♥, and your A♥), giving you a **flush** (A♥ 6♥ + 2♥ 4♥ from the board and any other heart — but wait: only four hearts are present: 2♥, 4♥, 6♥, A♥. The 7♠, 8♦, 8♠ are not hearts. So your flush is **A-6-4-2 of Hearts**, which is a **four-card flush**, but **not five cards** — wait! Wait: you have two hole cards and five board cards. Let's reevaluate.\n\nWait — correction: the board is **five cards**: 2♥, 4♥, 8♦, 8♠, 7♠. That’s all five community cards. So this is **post-river**.\n\nYou have: A♥ 6♥  \nBoard: 2♥, 4♥, 8♦, 8♠, 7♠\n\nSo your hand uses: A♥, 6♥, 2♥, 4♥ — that’s **four hearts**. But you need five for a flush. The fifth card on the board is 7♠ — not a heart. So only four hearts total on the table including yours. Therefore, **you do NOT have a flush**.\n\nBest possible hand with your cards:\n\n- Your highest card is Ace.\n- You have no pair (your A and 6 don’t connect with board pairs).\n- Board has:\n  - A pair of 8s (8♦ 8♠)\n  - No three-of-a-kind\n  - No straight: 8, 8, 7, 4, 2 — closest is 8-7-4-2 or 7-6-4-2-A? You have a 6, so 6-4-2-A is there, but missing 3 or 5 for a straight. 8-7-6-5-4 would be a straight, but no 5. So no straight.\n  - No flush (only four hearts).\n\nSo your best hand: **Ace-high**, using your A♥. The board already has a pair of 8s, so the best hand anyone can make without improving is either two pair, trips, or better.\n\nBut you only have Ace-high **with no pair**. Your opponent likely has something stronger.\n\nBut let's consider: you’ve been betting aggressively — raised pre-flop (+200), flop (+100), turn (+100). Opponent called each time. Now on the river, you can check or bet again.\n\nHowever, the board is dangerous: paired 8s, possible draws completed. But you have **no made hand** — just ace high.\n\nBut wait: could you have a **backdoor flush draw**? Yes — you had two small suited cards, hit two more hearts on flop, but didn't complete the flush. Final heart count: 2♥, 4♥ on board + A♥, 6♥ in hand = four hearts. Not enough.\n\nSo your actual hand: **High Card Ace**, with side cards 8, 7, 6, 4.\n\nBut the board has **two pair**: 8s and... no, only one pair (8s). 7♠ and others are singletons. So board shows **one pair (8s)**.\n\nYour best five-card hand: 8, 8, 7, A, 6 → **One Pair (8s) with Ace kicker**.\n\nWait! Yes — you use the two 8s on board, plus your Ace as kicker. So you have **a pair of 8s, with Ace kicker**.\n\nSimilarly, if opponent has no better pair, you win.\n\nBut opponent has called all your bets. That suggests they have at least a decent hand.\n\nPossible hands they might have:\n- Higher pair (like A-x, K-x, maybe 77, 99, etc.)\n- Two pair (e.g., 8-7, 7-7, 8-4, etc.)\n- Completed straight or flush.\n\nBut let’s assess: board is 2♥ 4♥ 8♦ 8♠ 7♠\n\nPossible straights: 6-5-7-8-? No 5 or 9. 8-7-6-5-4 — you have 6 and 4, but no 5. So no straight possible unless someone has 5-6 or 5-9.\n\nFlushes: three spades (8♠, 7♠, and possibly another? Only two spades on board: 8♠ and 7♠. Third spade? No — 8♦, 2♥, 4♥ — so only two spades. So no flush possible.\n\nWait: 8♠ and 7♠ are spades — that’s two. No third spade. So no flush possible.\n\nHearts: 2♥, 4♥ — only two on board. You have two more, but no one else likely does.\n\nSo no flush on board.\n\nBut two pair possibilities:\n- 8-7 (someone holds 7x): makes two pair (8s and 7s)\n- 8-4, 7-4, etc.\n\nOr trips: someone holding 8-8 (unlikely), or 7-7.\n\nOr overpairs: like A-A, K-K, Q-Q, etc.\n\nGiven that you’ve bet every street and opponent has called, they likely have a strong hand.\n\nBut you have top kicker with Ace, and a pair of 8s via the board.\n\nHowever, you **do not have pocket Aces or a strong starting hand** — you started with A♥ 6♥, which is marginal.\n\nBut your aggression may have represented strength.\n\nNow on the river, after betting all previous streets, checking might look weak.\n\nBut betting more (bluffing) risks being called by worse or raised by better.\n\nYour valid actions: fold (-1), check (0), raise by 100 or more (up to all-in).\n\nBut you have 19,550 chips.\n\nOpponent has at least that much (since you're both starting with same, and you've only lost 450 so far).\n\nYou are in the lead cumulatively, so minimizing risk might be wise.\n\nBut poker is about maximizing expected value.\n\nLet’s evaluate options:\n\n1. **Check (0)**: \n   - Safe. Lets opponent bluff or value-bet weaker hands.\n   - If they have nothing, they might check back.\n   - If they have better hand, they might bet anyway.\n   - Given you’ve bet every round, checking now might induce a bluff.\n\n2. **Bet/raise (e.g., 100 or more)**:\n   - Continuing aggression might make opponent fold medium-strength hands (like weak 8s, 7s, etc.)\n   - But if they have two pair or better, they’ll call or raise.\n   - Risk: getting raised off the pot when you only have Ace kicker.\n\n3. **All-in (-2)**:\n   - Overkill. Pot size? Let's estimate:\n     - Pre-flop: you bet 200, opponent called → 400\n     - Flop: you bet 100, opponent called → +200 → 600\n     - Turn: you bet 100, opponent called → +200 → 800\n     - River: no bet yet.\n     - So pot is ~800.\n     - Betting 100 now is half-pot, all-in is huge overbet.\n     - Likely only called by better hands.\n\n4. **Fold (-1)**: \n   - Too weak. You have a pair of 8s with Ace kicker. Folding here gives up equity.\n\nBest move: **Check (0)**.\n\nWhy?\n- You've been aggressive throughout.\n- You have a decent one pair hand with good kicker.\n- Checking doesn't commit more chips.\n- It may induce a bluff from opponent who thinks you’re weak.\n- Or it lets you see showdown without inflating the pot.\n\nBetting again risks raising from stronger hands (like two pair, sets) and won’t fold out many worse hands — most worse hands might already fold earlier.\n\nBut since opponent called all three bets, they likely have *something*.\n\nSo checking keeps the pot manageable and may get a free showdown or a bluff.\n\nReason: You have a marginal one-pair hand with decent kicker. Opponent has shown willingness to call. Further aggression is unlikely to improve outcome and may cost more chips. Checking balances caution and showdown value.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
